### Installation

Quick start instructions for the setup and configuration of kueue using Helm.

#### Prerequisites

- [Helm](https://helm.sh/docs/intro/quickstart/#install-helm) 3.14 or newer
- (Optional) [Cert-manager](https://cert-manager.io/docs/installation/)

#### Installing the chart

##### Install chart using Helm v4+

Either clone the kueue repository:

```bash
$ git clone git@github.com:kubernetes-sigs/kueue.git
$ cd kueue/charts
$ helm install kueue kueue/ --create-namespace --namespace kueue-system
```

Or use the charts pushed to `oci://registry.k8s.io/kueue/charts/kueue`:

```bash
helm install kueue oci://registry.k8s.io/kueue/charts/kueue --version="0.20.8" --create-namespace --namespace=kueue-system
```

For more advanced parametrization of Kueue, we recommend using a local overrides file, passed via the `--values` flag. For example:

```yaml
controllerManager:
  featureGates:
    - name: TopologyAwareScheduling
      enabled: true
  replicas: 2
  manager:
    resources:
      limits:
        cpu: "2"
        memory: 2Gi
      requests:
        cpu: "2"
        memory: 2Gi
```

```bash
helm install kueue oci://registry.k8s.io/kueue/charts/kueue --version="0.20.8" \
  --create-namespace --namespace=kueue-system \
  --values overrides.yaml
```

You can also use the `--set` flag. For example, to enable a feature gate (e.g., `TopologyAwareScheduling`):

```bash
helm install kueue oci://registry.k8s.io/kueue/charts/kueue --version="0.20.8" \
  --create-namespace --namespace=kueue-system \
  --set "controllerManager.featureGates[0].name=TopologyAwareScheduling" \
  --set "controllerManager.featureGates[0].enabled=true"
```

##### Offline manifest generation

When generating manifests offline, set the target Kubernetes version explicitly so the chart can render version-dependent resources. For example, with Helm:

```bash
helm template kueue oci://registry.k8s.io/kueue/charts/kueue --version="<FIXED_CHART_VERSION>" \
  --kube-version v1.36.0 --set enableMutatingAdmissionPolicy=true
```

For Kustomize's Helm integration with Helm v4, use Kustomize v5.8.1 or later and this `kustomization.yaml`:

```yaml
helmCharts:
  - name: kueue
    repo: oci://registry.k8s.io/kueue/charts
    version: <FIXED_CHART_VERSION>
    kubeVersion: v1.36.0
    releaseName: kueue
    valuesInline:
      enableMutatingAdmissionPolicy: true
```

Replace `<FIXED_CHART_VERSION>` with a chart release containing this fix, set the target version to the Kubernetes version of the destination cluster, and run `kustomize build --enable-helm .`. Helm or Kustomize must first be able to retrieve the chart; for offline Kustomize builds, prefetch it under `charts/kueue-<FIXED_CHART_VERSION>/kueue`.

##### Verify that controller pods are running properly.

```bash
$ kubectl get deploy -n kueue-system
NAME                           READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE
kueue-controller-manager       1/1     1            1           7s
```

##### Cert Manager

Kueue has support for third-party certificates.
One can enable this by setting `enableCertManager` to true.
By default the chart creates a self-signed `Issuer`. To reuse an existing
`Issuer` or `ClusterIssuer`, set `certManager.issuerRef`, for example:

```yaml
enableCertManager: true
certManager:
  issuerRef:
    group: cert-manager.io
    kind: ClusterIssuer
    name: my-cluster-issuer
```

If you reference a namespace-scoped `Issuer`, it must already exist in the
same namespace as the Helm release.
The referenced issuer must provide the CA data required by Kueue's cert-manager
integration, including `ca.crt` in the generated Secrets and the CA bundle used
for webhook and visibility API injection.

This will use certManager to generate a secret, inject the CABundles and set up the TLS.

Check out the [site](https://kueue.sigs.k8s.io/docs/tasks/manage/productization/cert_manager/)
for more information on installing cert manager with our Helm chart.

##### Prometheus

Kueue supports Prometheus metrics.
Check out the [site](https://kueue.sigs.k8s.io/docs/tasks/manage/productization/prometheus/)
for more information on installing kueue with metrics using our Helm chart.

### Configuration

#### Manager configuration

When `managerConfig.mode` is omitted, a supplied
`managerConfig.controllerManagerConfigYaml` takes precedence as a
complete configuration, including an explicitly empty string. Otherwise,
`managerConfig.structuredOverrides` merges with chart defaults. The manager and
job webhooks use the same resolved configuration. Null values for `mode`,
`controllerManagerConfigYaml`, or `structuredOverrides` are treated as absent
when selecting the configuration.

| Inputs (mode omitted) | Behavior |
|---|---|
| Neither supplied | Use chart defaults. |
| Only `structuredOverrides` | Merge overrides with chart defaults. An empty map uses defaults. |
| `controllerManagerConfigYaml` supplied | Use the YAML as a complete replacement; ignore structured overrides. |
| `controllerManagerConfigYaml: ""` | Keep the legacy empty configuration: Kueue uses its built-in defaults, not the chart defaults. Certificate-management settings still apply. |

Select an input explicitly with optional `managerConfig.mode`:

| Mode | Behavior |
|---|---|
| Omitted or `null` | Use the precedence above. |
| `structured` | Merge structured overrides with chart defaults; ignore YAML. |
| `yaml` | Use supplied YAML, including `""`; otherwise use chart defaults. Ignore structured overrides. |

Wrapper charts can leave mode unset so their consumers' YAML customizations
continue to take precedence over the wrapper's structured settings.

For structured configuration, specify only your customizations:

```yaml
managerConfig:
  structuredOverrides:
    clientConnection:
      qps: 600
    integrations:
      frameworks: ["batch/job", "pod"]
```

Maps merge recursively, lists and scalars replace, and `null` removes fields
inside `structuredOverrides`. A removed field falls back to Kueue's built-in
default, which can differ from the chart default. A replaced list, such as
`integrations.frameworks`, no longer picks up entries that later chart versions
add to its default. For removals during upgrades, follow
[Removing overrides during upgrades](#removing-overrides-during-upgrades) below.
When structured configuration is selected, absent, null, or empty
`structuredOverrides` uses chart defaults when no saved overrides are reused.
The two inputs are never merged together. Existing certificate-management
defaults still apply when `internalCertManagement` is not set.

The active defaults are defined only in
[templates/manager/_config.tpl](templates/manager/_config.tpl). They stay separate
from saved user values so settings without an override in the effective Helm
values follow the selected chart version's defaults. Reuse flags retain
previously saved overrides, even when omitted from the new values file.

Tools that read the default YAML string directly from `values.yaml` must instead
read the rendered manager ConfigMap. Full YAML configuration remains supported;
existing YAML users do not need to migrate.

##### Migrating to structured overrides

Copy intentional customizations from your YAML into `structuredOverrides`.
The YAML settings are not converted automatically.

To migrate while retaining other saved Helm values, explicitly supply
`managerConfig.mode: structured` along with your overrides:

```yaml
managerConfig:
  mode: structured
  structuredOverrides:
    clientConnection:
      qps: 600
```

```sh
helm upgrade kueue <chart> --namespace kueue-system \
  --reset-then-reuse-values --values values.yaml
```

Use `--reset-then-reuse-values` rather than
`--reuse-values` when moving to a new chart version. `--reuse-values` also keeps
the previous chart version's defaults, which can include its default YAML
configuration and its image tag.

Structured mode ignores retained YAML. Keep the mode in your values file for
subsequent upgrades. To switch back, explicitly set `mode: yaml` and supply the
full YAML configuration; omitting mode from a file does not clear a saved mode
when reusing values.

To drop the old chart's inherited YAML default while reusing values, you can
also set `controllerManagerConfigYaml: null` alongside your structured overrides.
Helm may retain this null in saved release values; subsequent upgrades treat it
as absent. Unlike null, `controllerManagerConfigYaml: ""` still selects an empty
YAML configuration. Removing explicitly saved settings is subject to Helm's
value-merging rules; see [Removing overrides during upgrades](#removing-overrides-during-upgrades).

Alternatively, leave mode unset, remove the `controllerManagerConfigYaml` key,
and use `--reset-values` with a values file containing all customizations you
want to retain, including settings outside `managerConfig`. Setting YAML to
`""` still selects an empty YAML configuration, not structured overrides.
Ensure that a parent chart or another values file does not supply the YAML key.

Without explicit structured mode, any `--reuse-values` upgrade can carry
the old chart's default YAML even if you never supplied it, causing structured
overrides to be ignored. Supply mode in your upgrade values; new wrapper defaults
alone may not take effect with reuse. Mode does not change Helm's handling of
omitted overrides or `null`; see
[Removing overrides during upgrades](#removing-overrides-during-upgrades).
When Kueue is a dependency, nest the settings under its dependency name or alias.
After an install or upgrade of this chart, Helm prints notes that warn when
structured overrides or the YAML configuration are ignored.
When Kueue is a dependency, Helm prints these notes only with
`--render-subchart-notes`.

| Subsequent upgrade options | Behavior |
|---|---|
| `--reuse-values` | Reuse the previous release's Helm values, including its chart defaults, then merge new values. This can retain the old chart's YAML default. Omitted saved settings remain. For removals, follow the instructions below. |
| `--reset-then-reuse-values` | Start from the new chart's defaults, then merge saved settings and new values. This drops an old chart's YAML default, but retains YAML explicitly saved by the user. Omitted saved settings remain. For removals, follow the instructions below. |
| `--reset-values` | Use chart defaults and the supplied values. Include all customizations you want to retain. |
| No reuse or reset flag | Helm retains saved values if no new values are supplied; supplying nonempty new values replaces the saved inputs. |

These flags control Helm values. When structured configuration is selected,
settings without an override in the effective values still follow the target
chart's manager configuration defaults.

Helm rejects unknown keys directly under `managerConfig`. The only allowed keys
are `mode`, `structuredOverrides`, and `controllerManagerConfigYaml`. Each accepts
`null`, meaning absent. Non-null mode must be `yaml` or `structured`. Non-null
configuration inputs must have the correct type even when not selected:
`structuredOverrides` is a map, and `controllerManagerConfigYaml` is a string.
Empty maps and empty YAML strings are valid. Fields inside the selected
configuration are validated by Kueue when it loads the configuration. Run
`helm lint <chart> --values values.yaml` before upgrading and review the rendered
configuration.

When upgrading, remove or correct unknown manager input keys and incorrectly
typed inputs. These values may previously have been ignored.

##### Removing overrides during upgrades

Reuse flags retain saved overrides omitted from your new values file. They can
also discard `null` removal instructions before the chart sees them.

Setting a saved override to `null` has a different effect with each upgrade option:

| Upgrade option | `null` over a saved override |
|---|---|
| `--reuse-values` | No effect: the saved value is still rendered. |
| `--reset-then-reuse-values` | Restores the chart default. |
| `--reset-values` | Removes the field. |

To remove an override or configuration field reliably, use `--reset-values` and
change `structuredOverrides` as follows:

| Intent | Change in `structuredOverrides` |
|---|---|
| Restore the chart default | Omit the override |
| Remove the rendered field | Set it to `null` |

Supply a values file containing **all customizations you want to retain**,
including those outside `managerConfig`, then run:

```sh
helm upgrade kueue <chart> --namespace kueue-system \
  --reset-values --values values.yaml
```

Keep `mode: structured` if you use it; otherwise omit `controllerManagerConfigYaml`.
Resetting values discards saved customizations not included in the file.

